|~ `::error`, the kind every error is a value of, and what is declared
   under it: the refusal of an error whose path is no vector of stops,
   the facts a refusal may carry, and the words of errors. The error
   `!|` opens is `::error{…}`, or the tag of the site that refused
   stacked over it, and its walk passes `::error` on its way to the map,
   so a word declared here reaches every error. ~|

:error
  |~~ The kind of errors. A reader learning a tool fails often, and
      a failure is the moment when the knowledge it needs is cheapest to
      give, so a failure is a value that carries it: the facts of the
      failure, the path it took, and the tag of the site that refused,
      which names the page of the procedure. An error is a map of facts
      under `::error`, `::error{:k 1}`, a value like any other until it
      is raised, and the bang of a literal raises it, `!{:k 1}`. Every
      refusal is a tag of its own, named for what failed,
      `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`, stacked over the error. `|` passes a
      raised error by, `map` stops on one, and `!|` opens it to that
      stack, so `!| type` reads the site, the verbs of this kind reach
      every error, and every step works on it as on any value: a fact is
      added and the error raised again, or it is recovered by the tag of
      its site. ::snippet~(::error | manifest) lists its verbs and refusals.
      ::link~(::explanation | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|

:error/opensAFailureLiteralToItsKind
  |~~ A failure literal opens to an `::error`. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:detail "boom"} !| type | eq ::error)

:error/standsOverItsKind
  |~~ Beneath the site's tag lies an `::error`. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| payload | type | eq ::error)

:error/readsItsFacts
  |~~ An error's facts read as fields. ~~|
  ::law~(::error{:k 1} | /k | eq 1)

:error/takesAFactOnTheWay
  |~~ A fact added on the fail track rides on. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| /request | eq "r-1")

:error/recoversByItsType
  |~~ A failure of a known type recovers. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)

:error/raisesAgainOtherwise
  |~~ A failure of another type raises again as itself. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:error/TrailNotVecError
  |~~ `:trail` among the facts of an error is its path, a
      vector of stops the runtime writes, `{:step :subject :skipped}`,
      from the step where it arose to the step where it is read.
      An error literal or a `raise` that writes a value that is no plain
      vector under `:trail`, a set, a quote and a doc among them, is
      refused as it makes the error. A `raise`
      that writes the `:trail` it read goes on with that path, and one
      that leaves it out starts a path at its own step.
      ::link~(::error | doc)
  ~~|

:error/TrailNotVecError/refusesANumberForATrail
  |~~ A number given as a trail is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:kind :oops :trail 5} !| [type /actualType] | eq [::error/TrailNotVecError ::number])

:error/TrailNotVecError/refusesATrailLaidOverAFailure
  |~~ A trail of a number laid over an error is refused when it is raised. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| union {:trail 5} | raise !| type | eq ::error/TrailNotVecError)

:error/TrailNotVecError/startsATrailAtTheRaise
  |~~ An error raised without a trail starts its trail at the raise. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| minus #[:trail] | raise | add 1
    !| /trail | map ~/step | eq [~(raise)])

:error/TrailNotVecError/refusesASetAQuoteAndADoc
  |~~ A set, a quote and a doc are no plain vector of stops. ~~|
  ::law~([#[1] ~(a) |~~ d ~~|] | map ~({:trail /} | raise !| type) | eq [::error/TrailNotVecError ::error/TrailNotVecError ::error/TrailNotVecError])

:error/name
  |~~ The name a reader asked for, a keyword or a tag, where nothing answers it. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::tag]

:error/addresses
  |~~ The addresses where a word of that name lives, one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
  ::set

:error/nearest
  |~~ The names nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:error/index
  |~~ The place of the element refused, counted from zero. ~~|
  ::number

:error/actualType
  |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~|
  ::tag

:error/places
  |~~ How many places the parts of the word read, where the call gave
      more values.
  ~~|
  ::number

:error/actualValue
  |~~ The value refused, where it is not the subject that the first stop
      of the trail holds.
  ~~|
  ::any

:error/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:error/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:error/field
  |~~ The field that does not fit, null when the payload is no map. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::null]

:error/printed
  |~~ The text the step prints as, which reads back as another step or as none. ~~|
  ::string

:error/trail
  |~~ Answers the path of the error, a vector of stops from the step
      that raised it to the `!|` that opened it, each a map of the `:step`, the `:subject` that step met, and the
      steps `:skipped` after it: `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail` answers
      the one stop of `add 1`, which met `"x"` and skipped `mul 2`. A
      step that ran the step that raised adds its stop after it, the `map`
      that ran an element among them, so the path reads outward:
      `[1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | map ~/step` answers
      `[~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))]`. The subject is an error opened by `!|`:
      under `|` a raised error passes `trail` by, as it passes every
      step, and a value that is no error is refused, a map with the key
      `:trail` among them.
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) ::link~(::error | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(/trail)

:error/trail/answer
  |~~ The stops of the path the error took. ~~|
  ::vec

:error/trail/SubjectNotErrorError
  |~~ `trail` reads the path of an error opened by `!|`, `"x" | add 1 !|
      trail`; the key `:trail` of a plain map is read by `/trail`.
  ~~|

:error/trail/holdsTheStepThatRaised
  |~~ The trail of an error raised at once holds the one step. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| trail | count | eq 1)

:error/trail/meetsTheSubjectOfTheStep
  |~~ The stop holds the subject the step met. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| trail | first | /subject | eq "x")

:error/trail/namesTheStepsSkipped
  |~~ The stop of the step that raised names the steps skipped after it. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | first | /skipped | eq ~(mul 2))

:error/trail/readsOutward
  |~~ The step that ran an element adds its stop after the element's. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | map ~/step | eq [~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))])

:error/trail/SubjectNotErrorError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map with the key `:trail` is no error. ~~|
  ::law~({:trail []} | trail !| [type /actualType] | eq [::error/trail/SubjectNotErrorError ::map])
